for the last two decades the MOBO Awards  have been a celebration of music of  black origin but for its founder Kanye  King it's been a relentless battle  against systemic racism within the music  industry not many black executives at a  senior level in the music industry you  know I would like to see their ethnicity  pay gap reporting mandatory unless we  feel comfortable having those  discussions about inequalities erase  progress will not be made musician Nadia  rose was signed to a major label and  says the lack of diversity there had a  huge effect on her career and would  always feel like my white male  counterparts that was science and the  label would always have you know I think  the budgets were great are they they  related to the ANR's there and everyone  knew how to market these particular  artists because they could relate and  then therefore they was capitalizing off  of them but then you're you're leaving  out the black women that you don't  understand where to put or how to push  and it just it wasn't a very comfortable  situation to be in according to the  latest diversity report in 2018 black  and ethnic minority representation in  the music industry as a whole was 17 0.8  percent it was higher among young  workers at twenty five point nine  percent but Bame representation among  senior managers was still only just over  18 percent earlier this month leading  names from the music industry took part  in a blackout Tuesday amid calls that  the time for genuine change is long  overdue the industry body UK music  acknowledges there are problems and will  be publishing its own plan of action in  the coming weeks I think those who sit  on boards should voluntarily leave  that's a really strong sign of action  and make space for individuals who  deservedly should be in those positions  kanya King says racism is the worst  pandemic we're facing but she also  believes this is a watershed moment and  change is still possible  Lucy Casa Sky News
 

